WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.562
The module challenge asks you to build one analytical query for a decision-maker.

00:00:04.562 --> 00:00:11.422
The question is which sales categories generated meaningful revenue and how much each qualifying category

00:00:11.422 --> 00:00:14.977
contributed. Start by defining one output row as one category.

00:00:14.977 --> 00:00:18.602
Then choose at least two useful aggregate measures.

00:00:18.602 --> 00:00:26.092
The challenge also requires a row-level condition in WHERE and a group-level condition in HAVING.

00:00:26.092 --> 00:00:32.522
Make sure you can explain why each condition belongs where you placed it.

00:00:32.522 --> 00:00:35.902
Finally, sort the result so the most important categories are easy to see.

00:00:35.902 --> 00:00:39.207
Do not focus only on producing SQL that runs.

00:00:39.207 --> 00:00:45.142
Write two short sentences explaining what the result means and how the row filter differs from the group

00:00:45.142 --> 00:00:50.737
filter. That explanation is part of the analytical work.

00:00:50.737 --> 00:00:55.032
Connect this idea to a dashboard summarizing admissions by department and month.

00:00:55.032 --> 00:00:59.802
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:59.802 --> 00:01:08.042
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:01:08.042 --> 00:01:12.487
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:01:12.487 --> 00:01:19.124
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
